sections = [
{"h2": "What is Flathead County, Montana, and how is it laid out?",
 "html": "<p>Flathead County is a large northwest Montana county with Kalispell as its seat, and it reaches north to the Canadian border. The <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flathead_County,_Montana\">Wikipedia entry</a> gives its total area as 5,256 square miles, of which 5,088 are land, and says it was created in 1893. The 2020 census counted 104,357 people.</p><p>The incorporated cities are Columbia Falls, Kalispell and Whitefish. Census-designated places include Bigfork, Lakeside, Somers, Martin City, Hungry Horse, West Glacier, Polebridge, Kila and Marion. The county holds much of Flathead Lake, the Flathead Valley and the Flathead River, and the western part of Glacier National Park lies inside it. Portions of the Flathead, Kootenai and Lolo National Forests are in the county as well.</p><p>It borders British Columbia to the north and shares lines with Glacier, Pondera, Teton, Lewis and Clark, Powell, Missoula, Lake, Sanders and Lincoln Counties. For a seller, that geography means very different properties under one county name: in-town Kalispell houses, lakeshore homes near Lakeside and Bigfork, and forested acreage near the park.</p>"},
{"h2": "What drives the Flathead County economy?",
 "html": "<p>Healthcare is the largest employment sector in Flathead County, and tourism is the other major driver. The Kalispell Chamber of Commerce lists Logan Health as the region's primary healthcare provider, and says the county's top industries are healthcare, manufacturing and tourism. A city economic development chapter for Kalispell also names retail trade and construction among the largest contributors.</p><p>Glacier National Park and the lake bring seasonal visitors, which in turn shapes the housing market in two ways. Some owners hold second homes or vacation rentals, and some buyers purchase specifically to use a property for stays. Montana's tax treatment of those properties, covered below, differs sharply from the treatment of a primary residence.</p><p>Logan Health describes itself as a nonprofit, 577-bed health system, so a steady pool of hospital and clinic workers also looks for homes in the valley. Sellers relocating for work often bring tight dates, and that is one reason cash sales appeal to them.</p>"},
{"h2": "Is wildfire a concern for a Flathead County house sale?",
 "html": "<p>Yes. The Wildfire Risk to Communities project says Flathead County has a very high risk of wildfire, higher than 92 percent of counties in the United States. The county also has a 2021 Community Wildfire Protection Plan, published through the Montana Department of Natural Resources and Conservation, which uses the term wildland urban interface for places where homes meet burnable vegetation.</p><p>For a house in the forested parts of the county, a buyer or an insurer may ask about defensible space, roofing and access for fire crews.</p><ul><li>Clear dry vegetation and woodpiles from around the house before a showing.</li><li>Keep records of any fuels reduction work done on the lot.</li><li>Ask your insurer for a written statement of current coverage, because buyers will ask.</li></ul>"},
{"h2": "How does the 2026 Montana tax structure treat Flathead County homes?",
 "html": "<p>A primary residence or long-term rental pays 0.76 percent on the first $378,000 of market value, 0.90 percent on value up to $756,000, 1.10 percent up to $1,511,999 and 1.90 percent beyond, while second homes and short-term rentals pay a flat 1.90 percent. The Department of Revenue explains the structure in its <a href=\"https://revenue.mt.gov/property/property-tax-changes/2026-property-tax-information\">2026 property owner guide</a>. The page lists second homes, VRBOs, Airbnbs and cabins among the flat-rate properties, and vacant residential lots too.</p><p>That gap is large in a county with lake and park properties. As an illustration of the taxable-value step only, the Census median owner-occupied value shown earlier, $467,700, would be 0.76 percent on $378,000 ($2,872.80) plus 0.90 percent on the remaining $89,700 ($807.30), for about $3,680 of taxable value. The same value taxed at the flat second-home rate would be about $8,886. The mills for your district then turn that taxable value into a bill.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Use of the property</th><th>2026 rate on the portion of value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Principal residence, first $378,000</td><td>0.76 percent</td></tr><tr><td>Principal residence, $378,001 to $756,000</td><td>0.90 percent</td></tr><tr><td>Principal residence, $756,001 to $1,511,999</td><td>1.10 percent</td></tr><tr><td>Second home, short-term rental, cabin</td><td>1.90 percent flat</td></tr><tr><td>Residence on qualified agricultural land that is not a primary residence</td><td>1.35 percent flat</td></tr></tbody></table><p>If you are selling a house you have rented short term, tell the buyer which rate applied to you. A buyer who plans to live there can enroll in the homestead program, and the department says the new owner must submit an application by March 1 to keep the reduced rate the following year.</p>"},
{"h2": "How does the Flathead County Treasurer collect and what is the delinquency timeline?",
 "html": "<p>The Flathead County Property Tax Office, at 290 A North Main Street in Kalispell, prepares and mails the tax bills, processes payments and works with the state on valuation. Bills go to the last known address at least 30 days before the November 30 due date, and the county's online portal shows 2026 real estate bills. You can reach the office at (406) 758-5680.</p><p>The county says online payments by debit or credit card carry a 3 percent fee, while an eCheck has no fee. Its delinquency page lays out the sequence plainly.</p><ul><li>November 30: first half due. December 1: first half becomes delinquent.</li><li>May 31: second half due. June 1: second half becomes delinquent.</li><li>Late June: notice of a pending tax lien is published.</li><li>Early August: the lien is attached to delinquent properties; the treasurer must attach it no later than the first working day in August when notice has been given.</li></ul><p>Interest runs at 5/6 of 1 percent per month plus a one-time 2 percent penalty on the unpaid balance. The county says a tax lien does not immediately cost you the property, but it begins a legal process. Lien certificates can be assigned to a purchaser who pays the exact delinquent amount, which is one more reason to clear taxes before you sell.</p>"},
{"h2": "What can you do if you think the valuation is wrong?",
 "html": "<p>Contact the Montana Department of Revenue first, because the county page says the department determines taxable value, including appraisal, classification, reductions and exemptions. The department's number listed for Flathead County is (406) 758-5700. You then have 30 days from the date on your classification and appraisal notice to file Form AB-26 for an informal review or file directly with the county clerk and recorder for a county tax appeal board hearing.</p><p>Flathead County's protest form is meant for use only after an active appeal with the Department of Revenue. Under Montana law, you protest in writing with the county treasurer and pay the disputed tax on time, otherwise you may not qualify for a refund and interest if the value is lowered. The Montana Tax Appeal Board and then district court are the later steps. Details are on the department's <a href=\"https://revenue.mt.gov/property/appraisal/appeals-and-reviews\">appeals page</a>.</p><p>When you present a case, the department accepts comparable sales near the January 1, 2024 valuation date, a fee appraisal and your own value estimate, among other items.</p>"},
]
faqs = [
{"q": "How can I pay Flathead County property tax online?",
 "a": "Use the county's online tax portal. A debit or credit card adds a 3 percent fee, while an eCheck has no fee. Enter only your assessor number to search."},
{"q": "Is wildfire risk high in Flathead County?",
 "a": "Yes, according to the Wildfire Risk to Communities project, which rates Flathead County's wildfire risk higher than 92 percent of U.S. counties. Insurance and defensible space often come up in a sale."},
{"q": "What is the Flathead County second-half tax deadline?",
 "a": "May 31, with delinquency beginning June 1. Interest then runs at 5/6 of 1 percent monthly plus a 2 percent penalty."},
{"q": "Will a tax lien take my Flathead County house?",
 "a": "A lien alone does not take the property. It is the county's claim for unpaid tax, penalty and interest, and it can be assigned to a purchaser who pays the delinquent amount. Paying the balance before or at closing clears it."},
{"q": "Can I sell a Flathead County house as-is for cash?",
 "a": "Yes. A cash buyer can purchase a house with deferred repairs or back taxes, and the title company pays the tax from your proceeds."},
]
